---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout title="A Depression-Circuit Enriched GPCR for Targeted Pharmacotherapy | Shabel Lab">
    <!-- Back Button -->
    <div class="navigation-aid">
        <a href="/research" class="back-link">&larr; Back to All Research</a>
    </div>

    <!-- Deep Dive Content -->
    <article class="research-detail">
        <h1>A Depression-Circuit Enriched GPCR for Targeted Pharmacotherapy</h1>
        
        <!-- Replaced placeholder with a semantic figure and caption -->
        <figure class="featured-figure">
            <img src="/images/GraphsExp3.jpg" alt="Diagram showing GPR151 enrichment in the habenula" class="featured-image" />
            <figcaption>Fig. 3. A, GPR151 mRNA expression in the habenula (outlined with dashed, red line). Scale, 500 um. B, GPR151 enrichment in the habenula is higher than the enrichment of any other GPCR in any other brain region (analysis of data from the Allen Brain Institute). </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <h2>Project Overview</h2>
        <p>Inflammation-associated depression is a clinically important and often treatment-resistant subtype of major depressive disorder. We identified GPR151, an orphan G protein-coupled receptor exceptionally enriched in the habenula (Fig. 3A,B), as a highly promising therapeutic target for this condition. We found that GPR151 shows conserved topography and maintains similar expression relationships with other habenular markers in both mice and humans. In mice, GPR151 deletion reduced passive coping following an inflammatory challenge in both sexes, despite preserving normal peripheral sickness and immune activation. Furthermore, restoring GPR151 expression in the adult male habenula successfully increased inflammation-induced amotivation without exacerbating weight loss or immune activation, though interestingly, this same manipulation was insufficient in females.</p>
        <p>In this project, our lab seeks to dissect this highly specific receptor pathway using state-of-the-art methodology. We aim to determine exactly how GPR151 drives inflammation-associated depressive behaviors, to uncover the divergent circuit-level mechanisms of action between males and females, and to definitively evaluate its viability as a targeted pharmacotherapy.</p>

        <h2>Methodology</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Cross-Species Mapping:</strong> Comparing GPR151 topography and expression relationships alongside other habenular markers in both murine and human tissue (Fig. 3A,B).</li>
            <li><strong>Genetic & Viral Modeling:</strong> Utilizing whole-body GPR151 deletion and targeted viral restoration in the adult habenula to isolate the receptor's neurological influence from general physiological responses.</li>
            <li><strong>Behavioral Assays:</strong> Measuring passive coping and amotivation following inflammatory challenges to accurately model treatment-resistant depression subtypes.</li>
            <li><strong>Physiological Monitoring:</strong> Tracking variables like peripheral sickness, weight loss, and systemic immune activation to ensure observed behavioral shifts are neurologically driven rather than secondary to physical illness.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Current Objectives</h2>
        <p>To gain precise spatial and temporal control over this receptor, we are currently breeding a newly generated line of floxed GPR151 mice. Using this model, we will test whether the highly specific removal of GPR151 directly within the habenula affects a subject's affective bias and alters their sensitivity to the amotivational effects of chronic stress.</p>
    </article>
</Layout>

<style>
    .navigation-aid {
        margin-bottom: 2rem;
    }

    .back-link {
        color: var(--accent-gray);
        text-decoration: none;
        font-weight: 600;
        transition: color 0.2s ease;
    }

    .back-link:hover {
        color: var(--primary-color);
    }

    .research-detail {
        max-width: 800px; /* Constrains the width to make reading long paragraphs easier on the eyes */
        margin: 0 auto; /* Centers the article on the page */
        background-color: #ffffff;
        padding: 3rem;
        border-radius: 8px;
        box-shadow: 0 4px 6px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.05);
    }

    .research-detail h1 {
        color: var(--primary-color);
        font-size: 2.2rem;
        margin-top: 0;
        margin-bottom: 2rem;
    }

    /* 1. Figure Container */
    .featured-figure {
        margin: 0 0 2.5rem 0; /* Removes default browser margin and adds bottom spacing */
    }

    /* 2. The Actual Image */
    .featured-image {
        width: 100%;
        height: auto; /* Keeps the aspect ratio from stretching */
        border-radius: 8px;
        display: block; /* Prevents a tiny gap from appearing below the image */
    }

    /* 3. The Caption Text */
    .featured-figure figcaption {
        margin-top: 0.85rem;
        font-size: 0.95rem;
        color: var(--text-muted);
        text-align: center;
        font-style: normal;
        line-height: 1.5;
        padding: 0 1rem; /* Keeps long captions from hitting the very edge of the screen */
    }

    .research-detail h2 {
        color: var(--text-main);
        font-size: 1.5rem;
        margin-top: 2rem;
        margin-bottom: 1rem;
        border-bottom: 2px solid var(--bg-page);
        padding-bottom: 0.5rem;
    }

    .research-detail p {
        font-size: 1.1rem;
        margin-bottom: 1.2rem;
        line-height: 1.7;
    }

    .research-detail ul {
        font-size: 1.1rem;
        line-height: 1.7;
        margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
        padding-left: 1.5rem;
    }

    .research-detail li {
        margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
    }

    /* ========================================== */
    /* MOBILE RESPONSIVENESS FIXES                */
    /* ========================================== */
    @media (max-width: 768px) {
        .research-detail {
            padding: 1.5rem; /* Cut the container padding in half to give text more width */
        }

        .research-detail h1 {
            font-size: 1.8rem; /* Scale down the main title so it fits nicely on a narrow screen */
            margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
        }

        .research-detail h2 {
            font-size: 1.35rem; /* Slightly scale down subheadings */
        }

        .featured-figure figcaption {
            padding: 0; /* Remove side padding on mobile since the container itself now has less padding */
            font-size: 0.85rem; /* Slightly shrink caption text to save space */
        }

        .research-detail p, .research-detail ul {
            font-size: 1rem; /* Marginally reduce standard text size to improve readability on phones */
        }
    }
</style>